    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d204">

                <pb facs="0349" n="253" sameAs="frus:frus1872p1/pg253"/>

                <head>No. 204.<lb/><hi rendition="#italic">Mr. Hunter to Mr. Francis.</hi></head>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Department of State</hi>,<lb/><hi rendition="#italic"
                                >Washington</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1872-07-30"><hi rendition="#italic">July</hi> 30,
                        1872</date>.</dateline>
                    <seg rendition="#float-left">No. 37.]</seg>
                </opener>
                <p><hi rendition="#small-caps">Sir</hi>: Your dispatch numbered 67, under date of
                    the 3d Instant, inclosing correspondence relative to the rumored shipment of
                    Greek criminals to the United States, is received, and the communication which
                    you addressed upon that subject to the secretary-general is approved. The
                    contradiction which has been given by the government of Greece to the rumor
                    referred to, as communicated in your dispatch No. 63, was, upon the receipt of
                    that dispatch, sent to the press for publication.</p>
                <p>I am, &amp;c.,</p>
                <closer>
                    <signed>W. HUNTER,<lb/><hi rendition="#italic">Acting Secretary.</hi></signed>
                </closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
